-- title:
--   Values along a two-ended annulus reduce to the node value
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field, $A \subseteq L$ a valuation subring, and $F$ a field extension of $L$ in which every nonzero element has a divisor of degree $0$ whose coefficient at each place $v$ is $v.\mathrm{ord}$ of that element; let $\overline{F}$ and $\overline{F}'$ be fields over the residue field of $A$. Let $\mu$ be a real absolute value on $L$ cutting out $A$, in the sense that $a \in A$ exactly when $\mu(a) \le 1$. Let $An, An'$ be annuli of $F$ over $A$ with the same domain of places and the same modulus $\pi$, with $(\pi : L) \ne 0$, and with parameters satisfying $An'.\mathrm{param} \cdot An.\mathrm{param} = \pi$ in $F$. Let $C$ be a component chart over $\overline{F}$ and $x$ a place of $\overline{F}$ over the residue field of $A$ with $An$ attached to $C$ at $x$: that is, $x$ is a node of $C$, the parameter of $An$ is $C$-integral with $\mathrm{ord}_x$ of its reduction equal to $1$, and for every $C$-integral $f$ with nonzero reduction and $P.\mathrm{ord}\,f = 0$ at all $P$ in $An.\mathrm{dom}$, the value $P.\mathrm{evalAt}\,f \cdot (P.\mathrm{evalAt}\,An.\mathrm{param})^{-\mathrm{ord}_x(\bar f)}$ lies in $A$ and is a unit there; assume moreover that the residue field of $x$ is generated by the residue field of $A$, i.e. $x$ is rational. Let likewise $An'$ be attached to a chart $C'$ over $\overline{F}'$ at a place $x'$. Assume the domain contains two places $Q_1, Q_2$ with $\mu(Q_1.\mathrm{evalAt}\,An.\mathrm{param}) \ne \mu(Q_2.\mathrm{evalAt}\,An.\mathrm{param})$. Let $g \in F$ satisfy $0 \le Q.\mathrm{ord}\,g$ for all $Q$ in $An.\mathrm{dom}$, be $C$-integral with nonzero $C$-reduction, and, for some $\kappa \in L$ with $\mu(\kappa) = 1$, let $\kappa^{-1} g$ be $C'$-integral with nonzero $C'$-reduction. Then for every place $R$ in $An.\mathrm{dom}$ the value $R.\mathrm{evalAt}\,g$ lies in $A$, its image in the residue field of $A$ equals $x.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of the $C$-reduction of $g$, and if $\mathrm{ord}_x$ of that reduction is $0$ then $R.\mathrm{evalAt}\,g$ is a unit of $A$.
--
--   This is the constancy statement underlying the reduction map of a closed annulus onto its special fibre: a function with no poles on the annulus which is integral with nonzero reduction at both ends takes, at every place of the annulus, a value reducing to its value at the node, and a unit value when the node order vanishes. It is obtained from the attachment conditions at the two ends together with the product formula for the orders of $g$ along the annulus, and is used in the subsequent analysis of values on annuli, in particular in locating the unique zero of a function whose node order is one and in the normalised evaluation results for functions with prescribed behaviour of $\mu$ on the annulus.
